{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-959bbc98-ce44-487e-b101-8ea8afcecc51","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b173e6afbd247bb7","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-im2hhlpv/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E001.md","content":"# Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic converts all reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator manager binary to structured key-value pairs so operators can query logs by resource name, namespace, and kind. It addresses the three diagnosed problems within the manager binary: message-embedded resource identity (converting e.g. `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Foo in namespace bar\")` to `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", foo.Name, \"namespace\", foo.Namespace, \"resourceKind\", \"Foo\")`), the DSCI controller's non-standard key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` migrated to `name`/`namespace`), and the missing `resourceKind` field across all controller tiers. Field naming follows camelCase to align with controller-runtime's existing structured log key conventions.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the manager binary using grep/static analysis; produce a coverage checklist of every call site and whether it embeds resource identity in the message string (this audit also confirms the strategy's 75% embedded-identity assumption).\n- Convert every reconciliation error log call in the manager binary that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n- Migrate the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller from non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`).\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to reconciliation error logs in all manager-binary controller tiers:\n  - DSC controller (DataScienceCluster) and the 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) — where kind is implied by controller name, this is a queryability enhancement added in the same pass.\n  - Module controller and its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator).\n  - 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) — kind is not implied by controller name for these.\n  - DSCI controller.\n- Add `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields to error logs in all 15 action files across 4 tiers: 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`), 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`), 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`), 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`).\n- Handle cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): emit `name` and `resourceKind`; `namespace` is either omitted or set to empty string, never a spurious value.\n- Author and verify the CI lint rule / static-analysis check covering the manager binary's converted call sites: it flags any `log.Error` call in the manager binary that embeds name/namespace in the message text or is missing the required structured fields.\n- Backwards-compatibility validation (per the strategy's NFR): before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error; deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack configured (Loki, CloudWatch), trigger reconciliation errors, and verify both formats are ingested without configuration changes; coordinate with SRE/Support to identify text-based alert rules that rely on the changed message strings and record the identified rules and needed updates.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Every reconciliation error log call in the manager binary that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string now uses structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), and the redundant identity is removed from the message text — measured by the coverage checklist (every audited call site accounted for) and the CI lint rule flagging zero remaining violations in the manager binary.\n- All DSCI controller structured field names use the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`); a grep of the codebase confirms no references to the old field names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization` as log keys) remain, and a unit test asserts DSCI error logs contain the standard field names.\n- For each service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup), a unit test parsing test logger output asserts `resourceKind` is present with the correct value on reconciliation errors.\n- Unit tests assert `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` are present with correct values in error logs across all 15 action files (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module).\n- Unit tests assert module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` across all 8 module handlers.\n- Unit tests assert DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind`.\n- A unit test confirms cluster-scoped resources do not emit a spurious namespace value (namespace omitted or empty string).\n- The CI lint rule / static-analysis check covering the manager binary's call sites is authored, committed, and passes on the converted codebase (zero violations), and fails on a deliberately non-compliant call site.\n- The before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error is recorded, showing structured fields present; the dev-cluster validation with the production log consumer stack confirms ingestion of both formats without configuration changes; the SRE/Support coordination on text-based alert rules is completed and its results recorded.\n- Existing unit and end-to-end tests in the manager binary pass with the log changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): convert message-embedded resource name/namespace to structured KV pairs — manager binary call sites.\n- HLR-2 (P0): DSCI controller migrated to camelCase key names.\n- HLR-3 (P0): service controller error logs include `resourceKind`.\n- HLR-4 (P0): all 15 action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`.\n- HLR-6 (P0): module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`.\n- HLR-7 (P1, bundled): DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` — bundled because it is added in the same mechanical pass over the same `log.Error` call sites and verified by the same lint rule; not separately schedulable.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":35378,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b10400d4ddc10d55","agent_id":"a94e80975625d5b70","session_id":"ddc88db0-c91c-4182-8c51-a0cdf64bc03d","uuid":"4376fbdc-c59a-49e0-b659-e85b4c4c7351","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:33:20.075Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
