{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-1ef257f9-c91b-4e20-9b57-993a4701e27a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b052a1ead3c885e8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-im2hhlpv/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E003.md","content":"# Document the structured log field convention and publish for external operator adoption\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic produces the documentation deliverables of the strategy: a contribution guideline that specifies the structured log field convention for controller development in the rhods-operator, the design doc with the three-problem diagnosis and before/after code examples, the DSCI key-migration guide for operators, and publication of the convention for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) per platform policy overlay 0008.\n\n## Scope\n\nContent outline (inside Scope per artifact contract):\n\n- **Contribution guideline** for new controller development in the rhods-operator, specifying:\n  - Required structured fields: `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`.\n  - camelCase field-naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime's existing structured log key conventions).\n  - The three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), missing `resourceKind`.\n  - Cluster-scoped resource handling (namespace omitted or empty string).\n  - How the CI lint rule enforces the convention.\n- **Design doc** with before/after code examples for converting message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs (the strategy flags this doc as \"to be created\").\n- **DSCI key-migration guide**: release-notes documentation of the migration from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace`, with before/after query examples for Loki/CloudWatch, noting the single-release transition intent.\n- **External publication**: publish the convention as a specification for voluntary adoption by the COO-owned operators (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator), and file tracking issues in the COO projects. Adoption remains at the external teams' discretion per platform policy — RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies.\n- **Open product decision to record in the guideline**: whether the convention extends beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category) is a pending PM/Observability decision; the guideline documents the three required fields and marks extras as a future extension pending that decision.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The contribution guideline exists, is committed to the rhods-operator repository, and specifies the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis.\n- The design doc includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs and a DSCI key-migration guide with before/after query examples.\n- The documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented conversion — a reviewer confirms the documented field names, examples, and controller-tier descriptions match the code as merged.\n- Release notes document the DSCI key-name migration with before/after query examples.\n- The convention is published as a specification for external operator adoption, and tracking issues are filed in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-8 (P1): document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":37826,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b10400d4ddc10d55","agent_id":"a94e80975625d5b70","session_