{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a8b692b6b13338d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\t**Strategy**: Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n13\t**Triage outcome**: Not below-threshold. Although the strategy is S-sized, single-component (rhods-operator), and its code work is largely mechanical, two escape conditions apply: (1) the scope spans architecturally distinct sub-systems — the manager binary and the cloudmanager binary are separate executables with separate controller sets, warranting separate PR review cycles; and (2) the strategy includes substantive content authoring as a distinct work stream (HLR-8: a new contribution guideline documenting the convention, plus publication for external operator adoption) in addition to code implementation. Per Step 0, below-threshold does not apply; full decomposition follows.\n14\t\n15\t## Epic List\n16\t\n17\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n18\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document the structured log field convention and publish for external operator adoption | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n22\t\n23\t## Dependency DAG\n24\t\n25\t```mermaid\n26\tgraph TD\n27\t  RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 --> RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n28\t  RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 --> RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\tBoth implementation epics are roots and run in parallel; the docs epic is blocked by all Implementation epics (Rule 11 — docs describe what was built).\n32\t\n33\t## DAG Justification\n34\t\n35\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n36\t|------|------|-----------|\n37\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repo, different binaries/areas (manager vs cloudmanager controllers). Merge-conflict/coordination risk only, not a dependency. Both carry P0 HLRs and can proceed independently. |\n38\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; the guideline documents the conversion actually performed in the manager binary. |\n39\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by ALL Implementation epics; the guideline and external publication reference the cloudmanager conversion and shared-reconciler behavior. |\n40\t\n41\tNo Investigation epics were created. All four open questions were evaluated against the decision rule (\"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\") and none qualifies:\n42\t\n43\t- **Additional fields beyond name/namespace/kind** (PM / Observability team): changes convention content at most, not epic existence or approach; the doc ships with the three required fields and notes extras as a future extension. Captured as an AC/note on E003 and a health warning.\n44\t- **Which alerts rely on message-string patterns** (SRE / Support): this is the log-consumer regression validation step itself — engineering work inside E001/E002 with SRE coordination, not a structural unknown. Captured as an AC (backwards-compatibility NFR).\n45\t- **Cloudmanager release timing** (PM): a scheduling/prioritization decision; both epics exist and their content is identical either way. Noted as a health warning; E002 remains independently schedulable.\n46\t- **Actual percentage of embedded log calls** (audit): the audit is the first step of the implementation (grep/static analysis to enumerate call sites); conversion proceeds the same way regardless of the resulting percentage. Captured as an AC (coverage checklist) on E001/E002.\n47\t\n48\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n49\t\n50\t| HLR | Priority | Epic |\n51\t|-----|----------|------|\n52\t| HLR-1 — convert message-embedded name/namespace to structured KV pairs (all rhods-operator reconciliation error logs) | P0 | E001 (manager binary call sites) + E002 (cloudmanager call sites) |\n53\t| HLR-2 — DSCI migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001 |\n54\t| HLR-3 — service controllers include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 |\n55\t| HLR-4 — all 15 action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 (all 15 action files live in the manager binary) |\n56\t| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controllers include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 |\n57\t| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | E001 |\n58\t| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 (bundled) — same `log.Error` call sites, same files, and the same CI lint rule as HLR-1's conversion; `resourceKind` on the framework controllers is added in the same mechanical pass, so a split would convert the same call sites twice and audit the same files twice. Not separately schedulable. |\n59\t| HLR-8 — document convention as contribution guideline and publish for external adoption | P1 | E003 |\n60\t\n61\tFull HLR coverage: all 8 HLRs map to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n62\t\n63\t## Component Mapping\n64\t\n65\tThe strategy's affected component is **rhods-operator** (manager + cloudmanager binaries), which is not a verbatim entry in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. The closest canonical component covering the RHOAI platform operator is **AI Core Platform** — E001 and E002 are assigned this component. E001 and E002 both span the strategy's single rhods-operator component; the per-epic split is along the binary/sub-system boundary (manager vs cloudmanager), not along components. E003 (contribution guideline + publication) is assigned the canonical **Documentation** component. These mappings are non-obvious and recorded here per Step 2.\n66\t\n67\trhods-operator is confirmed in the platform: `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md` exists and corroborates the controller tiers, the action-file architecture, the cloudmanager binary and its AWS/Azure/CoreWeave controllers, and controller-runtime/logr usage throughout. No new-component onboarding chain is needed.\n68\t\n69\t## Shared-Surface Enforcement Deliverable (CI Lint Rule)\n70\t\n71\tThe strategy's risk mitigation and acceptance criteria mandate a CI lint rule / static-analysis check over the converted `log.Error` call sites. Per the shared-surface rule, this is NOT a standalone work-type epic: each binary's epic authors and verifies the part of the lint rule covering its own call sites (see E001 and E002 Scope/ACs).\n72\t\n73\t## Health Warnings\n74\t\n75\t- **E001 size above the ~2-week guideline (managed)**: E001 is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (audit ~0.5 sprint + manager-binary conversion ~0.75–1 sprint). Kept whole because the conversion is one mechanical pass over the same binary's `log.Error` call sites and a split would audit the same files twice and duplicate the lint rule. Re-split trigger: the call-site audit exceeds the strategy's ~0.5-sprint estimate or finds materially more embedded call sites than the 75% assumption, pushing E001 past 2 sprints — at that point split by controller tier (component controllers + action files / module controller / service controllers + DSCI + DSC).\n76\t- **Unresolved open question — additional log fields** (owner: PM / Observability team): whether the convention adds fields beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` is a PM decision that affects only the guideline's content, not the code conversion. E003 documents the three required fields and flags extras as a pending product decision.\n77\t- **Cloudmanager release sequencing** (owner: PM / ODH Operator team): open question whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as manager. This is a scheduling decision, not a structural one; E002 is P0 by HLR-5 and independently schedulable, but a decision to defer it to a subsequent release would be a PM call that overrides the priority label at planning time.\n78\t- **DSCI query migration**: existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will break. Mitigation is captured as an AC on E001 (no old field names remain, unit-test coverage) and E003 (release notes with before/after query examples); the strategy intends a single-release transition to avoid long-term inconsistency.\n79\t- **Non-blocking priority observation**: HLR-7 is P1 while bundled into the P0 E001 — this is the priority-inheritance exception case (incidental to the same implementation surface), not an inversion.\n80\t\n81\t## Tiered Delivery\n82\t\n83\tNot applicable. The only external dependency (COO team adoption of Loki/Tempo Operator log formats) is explicitly out of scope per platform policy (overlay 0008): RHOAI publishes the convention for voluntary adoption and does not gate any deliverable on it. All strategy deliverables deliver value independently of the external teams' choices.\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac8f88166758eb95","agent_id":"a50ea2e05f464d44a","session_id":"ddc88db0-c91c-4182-8c51-a0cdf64bc03d","uuid":"5b46364d-c83d-45aa-a9ca-3e8d0d48d410","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:35:16.167Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a8b692b6b13338d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
