{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bedfe7008fd1f40e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource\n4\t  context\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: AI Core Platform\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 8\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Standardize cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs with structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic applies the same structured-logging standardization to the rhods-operator's cloudmanager binary, which handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies (deployed on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern, so they already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger; the remaining work is converting message-embedded resource identity in error paths and adding `resourceKind`.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the cloudmanager binary's controllers using grep/static analysis; produce a coverage checklist of every call site and whether it embeds resource identity in the message string.\n39\t- Convert every reconciliation error log call in the cloudmanager binary that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n40\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to reconciliation error logs in the AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine controllers.\n41\t- Handle cluster-scoped resources if present in these reconciliation paths: emit `name` and `resourceKind`; `namespace` is either omitted or set to empty string, never a spurious value.\n42\t- Author and verify the CI lint rule / static-analysis check covering the cloudmanager binary's converted call sites: it flags any `log.Error` call in the cloudmanager binary that embeds name/namespace in the message text or is missing the required structured fields.\n43\t- Backwards-compatibility validation (per the strategy's NFR): before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error, and verification that the production log consumers used on the non-OpenShift Kubernetes deployments ingest the new format without configuration changes.\n44\t\n45\t## Acceptance Criteria\n46\t\n47\t- Every reconciliation error log call in the cloudmanager binary that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string now uses structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — measured by the coverage checklist (every audited call site accounted for) and the CI lint rule flagging zero remaining violations in the cloudmanager binary.\n48\t- Unit tests assert that AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields with correct values, verified by parsing test logger output.\n49\t- A unit test confirms cluster-scoped resources in these paths do not emit a spurious namespace value (namespace omitted or empty string).\n50\t- The CI lint rule / static-analysis check covering the cloudmanager binary's call sites is authored, committed, and passes on the converted codebase (zero violations), and fails on a deliberately non-compliant call site.\n51\t- The before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error is recorded, showing structured fields present alongside the changed message text, and log consumer ingestion is verified without configuration changes.\n52\t- Existing unit tests in the cloudmanager binary pass with the log changes.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- HLR-1 (P0): convert message-embedded resource name/namespace to structured KV pairs — cloudmanager binary call sites.\n57\t- HLR-5 (P0): cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n58\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac8f88166758eb95","agent_id":"a50ea2e05f464d44a","session_id":"ddc88db0-c91c-4182-8c51-a0cdf64bc03d","uuid":"709e0548-f2b7-414d-985e-06c1ea2de022","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:35:19.333Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bedfe7008fd1f40e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
