{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8457c98ec30766d1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'Watch item, no action required: E001 is above the ~2-week guideline\n10\t    (~1.25-1.5 sprints) but is one logical change kept whole with a recorded estimate,\n11\t    why-not-split rationale, and a concrete re-split trigger in the decomposition\n12\t    Health Warnings (split by controller tier if the audit exceeds ~0.5 sprint or\n13\t    finds materially more embedded call sites) — defensible trade-off, do not split.'\n14\terror: null\n15\t---\n16\t## Review Summary\n17\t\n18\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n19\tRecommendation: accept\n20\t\n21\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exits 0 — epic body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and shared-surface enforcement deliverable placement all pass). Triage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition), so the 3-epic output is expected and not penalized.\n22\t\n23\tThe decomposition is a clean translation of the strategy: two parallel Implementation epics split along the manager/cloudmanager binary boundary, both feeding a Rule 11 docs-authoring epic. All 8 HLRs are traceable, the DAG matches frontmatter exactly, no Investigation epics were needed (each open question is correctly dispositioned as non-structural), and the CI lint rule is correctly folded per-surface into E001/E002 rather than carved out as a standalone work-type epic.\n24\t\n25\t## Criterion Details\n26\t\n27\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n28\tAll 8 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 splits across E001 (manager call sites) and E002 (cloudmanager call sites); HLR-2/3/4/6 → E001; HLR-5 → E002; HLR-7 → E001 (bundled); HLR-8 → E003. No P0 HLR is missing and the traceability matrix is present. Priority inheritance is correct: both blocking epics (E001, E002) are P0, and the P1 docs epic is the dependent, not a blocker (docs-authoring is also exempt from priority inheritance). HLR-7 (P1) bundled into the P0 E001 is not priority collapse: the matrix row carries an inline `(bundled)` rationale (same `log.Error` call sites, same files, same lint-rule pass, not separately schedulable), and `resourceKind` on DSC/component controllers is incidental to the same mechanical pass required by P0 HLR-3/4/6 — the bundling-recording shape is compliant. No P2 HLRs exist in the strategy.\n29\t\n30\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n31\tDAG is E001 → E003, E002 → E003; both implementation epics are parallel roots. Diagram edges match E003's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly (E001/E002 have empty dependencies), arrows point dependency → dependent, and no edge references a nonexistent epic. No circular dependencies. Critical path length 2 fits the S strategy heuristic (1-2). The E001 ∥ E002 parallelism is correct per Rule 15: same repo but separate binaries/controller sets — merge-conflict risk only, and each epic owns its own lint-rule portion, so there is no artifact dependency requiring an edge. The Rule 11 edges to the docs epic are valid and correctly not flagged for serialization. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable. No branch files exist, so branch/gated_by checks are not applicable. Cross-check of epic content found no data/artifact dependency missing from the DAG (the only shared surface, the CI lint rule, is deliberately per-binary by design and per the shared-surface rule must not be an edge or a standalone epic).\n32\t\n33\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n34\tAll epics are single-team (ODH Operator team) with canonical components (AI Core Platform for E001/E002, Documentation for E003 — both verified against `.context/rhai-components.txt`). The E001/E002 split is along the binary/sub-system boundary, which is the right seam for separate PR review cycles. No scope duplication: E001 and E002 convert disjoint call sites in disjoint binaries, and each authors only its own binary's lint-rule portion. No work-product type mixing — code implementation (E001/E002) is cleanly separated from content authoring (E003, including the design doc and DSCI migration guide). E001 is above the ~2-week guideline (~1.25-1.5 sprints by the strategy's own effort arithmetic), but this is the defensible shape: one logical change kept whole with a recorded estimate, a why-not-split rationale (a split would audit the same files twice and duplicate the lint rule), and a concrete conditional re-split trigger in Health Warnings (audit exceeds ~0.5 sprint or materially more embedded call sites → split by controller tier). Recorded as a watch item; criterion not docked and no split demanded.\n35\t\n36\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n37\tNo Investigation epics were created, and the justification correctly applies the decision rule to all four open questions — none changes epic existence or content structure (the audit is E001/E002's own first step; alert identification is engineering work; release sequencing is a PM scheduling call; the field-set question affects only E003's content and is captured there). E001 and E002 are Implementation epics producing concrete artifacts (converted code, unit tests, lint rule, validation records). E003 is Implementation with `implementation_type: docs-authoring`, which is the correct typing for a guideline/design-doc/publication deliverable. All `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null, consistent with no gates; no epic lists an Investigation dependency.\n38\t\n39\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n40\tAll three epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (not `investigation_signals`); all three ai-signals rationale files exist and their rationales match the frontmatter values. E001/E002 signal values are well justified: change_specificity +1 (exact files, paths, counts, field names, before/after examples), pattern_precedent +1 (framework controllers and `modules_controller_actions_inject_env.go` already emit structured fields; cloudmanager shared reconciler already provides name/namespace), existing_foundation +1, external_dependency +1 (COO adoption excluded), human_process_gates 0 (SRE coordination is input, not a sign-off gate). E003's divergent values are correctly calibrated: open_questions 0 (the additional-fields PM decision directly affects the guideline's content), external_dependency -1 (tracking issues in COO projects), human_process_gates -1 (mandatory technical review for docs-authoring). The `architecture_claims: 1` scores are correctly calibrated against `.context` — the strategy makes extensive architecture claims (5 controller tiers, 15 action files, DSCI key names, cloudmanager binary), the architecture-context file covers rhods-operator, and each rationale cites the corroborating file rather than claiming no architecture claims. No arithmetic or threshold checks performed (pipeline-computed).\n41\t\n42\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n43\tEvery epic has concrete, testable ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria and verification methods: coverage-checklist + lint-rule-zero-violations measurements for the conversion, unit tests parsing test logger output for field presence per controller tier and per action file, grep for removed DSCI key names, cluster-scoped namespace handling, and lint-rule failure demonstration on a deliberately non-compliant call site. Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC; no konflux-chain, replacement, or rollback-mandated epics exist (the DSCI key migration is a log-format change whose transition risk the strategy mitigates via release notes and single-release timing, both captured — relea